n functions}, author={Fan Chang}, year={2026}, eprint={2606.08958}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.08958}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA spectral correlation inequality for increasing Boolean functions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/406465302_A_spectral_correlation_inequality_for_increasing_Boolean_functions)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # A spectral correlation inequality for increasing Boolean functions ... DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2606.08958 ... To read the file of this research,\nPreprint\n\n# A spectral correlation inequality for increasing Boolean functions\n\n  * June 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2606.08958\n\nAuthors:\n\nFan Chang\n\nFan Chang\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nTalagrand's correlation inequality provides a quantitative strengthening of the Harris--Kleitman inequality for increasing Boolean functions. Motivated by a Fourier-analytic conjecture of Friedgut, Kahn, Kalai, and Keller, we prove that $ \\mathrm{Cov}(f,g)\\ge 2\\sum_{S\\neq\\emptyset}|S|\\hat f(S)^2\\hat g(S)^2 $ holds for all increasing Boolean functions $f,g:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}$. The proof combines the reverse Bonami--Beckner inequality with Young's convolution inequality. We also establish a sharp pointwise inequality: for every $n\\ge1$, every $0\\leρ\\le1$, and every $f,g:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to[0,1]$, the optimal constant $c_{ρ,n}$ for which $ \\left\\langle f,T_ρg \\right\\rangle\\ge c_{ρ,n}\\|f*g\\|_2^2 $ holds for all such f,g is 1 for $0\\leρ\\le1/2$, $(2(1-ρ))^n$ for $1/2<ρ<1$, and 0 for ρ=1. Integrating this pointwise inequality yields, for $n\\ge1$, the slightly improved bound \\mathrm{Cov}(f,g)\\ge 4\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{2n}\\sum_{S\\neq\\emptyset}|S|\\hat f(S)^2\\hat g(S)^2.\n\n## No file available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nRequest file PDF\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (14)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nOn the Correlation of Increasing Families\n\nArticle\n\n  * Nov 2015\n\n  * Gil Kalai\n  * Nathan Keller\n  * Elchanan Mossel\n\nThe classical correlation inequality of Harris asserts that any two monotone increasing families on the discrete cube are nonnegatively correlated. In 1996, Talagrand established a lower bound on the correlation in terms of how much the two families depend simultaneously on the same coordinates. Talagrand's method and results inspired a number of important works in combinatorics and probability theory. In this paper we present stronger correlation lower bounds that hold when the increasing families satisfy natural regularity or symmetry conditions. In addition, we present several new classes of examples for which Talagrand's bound is tight. A central tool in the paper is a simple lemma asserting that for monotone events noise decreases correlation. This lemma gives also a very simple derivation of the classical FKG inequality for product measures, and leads to a simplification of part of Talagrand's proof.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nFamilies of Non-disjoint subsets\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jun 1966\n\n  * Daniel J. Kleitman\n\nView\n\nEtudes des coefficients Fourier des fonctiones de Lp(G)\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 1970\n\n  * Aline Bonami\n\nView\n\nA lower bound for the critical probability in a certain percolation process\n\nArticle\n\n  * Oct 2008\n  * MATH PROC CAMBRIDGE\n\n  * T. E. Harris\n\nConsider a lattice L in the Cartesian plane consisting of all points (x, y) such that either x or y is an integer. Points with integer coordinates (positive, negative, or zero) are called vertices and the sides of the unit squares (including endpoints) are called links. Each link of L is assigned the designation active with probability p or passive with probability 1 − p, independently of all other links. Furthermore, we generalize our results to the correlation between monotone functions on [0, 1] n with respect to the Lebesgue measure.\n\nGeometric influences II: Correlation inequalities and noise sensitivity\n\nArticle\n\n  * Nov 2014\n  * ANN I H POINCARE-PR\n\n  * Nathan Keller\n  * Elchanan Mossel\n  * Arnab Sen\n\nHow much are increasing sets positively correlated?\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jun 1996\n  * COMBINATORICA\n\n  * Michel Talagrand\n\n\nInequalities in Fourier Analysis\n\nArticle\n\n  * Mar 1975\n  * ANN MATH\n\n  * William Beckner\n\nTHIS NOTE DESCRIBES TWO RESULTS: (i) a sharp Hausdorff-Young inequality for the Fourier transform on L(p)(R(n)) which extends an earlier result of Babenko; and (ii) a sharp form of Young's inequality for the convolution of functions on R(n). That is, best possible constants are obtained for the following L(p)(R(n)) inequalities: [Formula: see text].\n\n\nOptimal Young's convolutions inequality and its reverse form on the hypercube\n\n  * Jan 2025\n\n  * David Beltran\n  * Paata Ivanisvili\n  * José Madrid\n  * Lekha Patil\n\nDavid Beltran, Paata Ivanisvili, José Madrid, and Lekha Patil. Optimal Young's convolutions inequality and its reverse form on the hypercube. arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.06115, 2025.\n\n  * Jan 2025\n\n  * Fan Chang\n  * Yu Chen\n\nFan Chang and Yu Chen. Talagrand-type correlation inequalities for submodular and supermodular functions on the hypercube. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.22307, 2025.\n\nIntersecting families of edges in hypergraphs having the hereditary property\n\n  * Jan 1972\n  * 61-66\n\nV. Chvátal. Intersecting families of edges in hypergraphs having the hereditary property. In Hypergraph Seminar (Proc. First Working Sem., Ohio State Univ., Columbus, Ohio, 1972; dedicated to Arnold Ross), Lecture Notes in Math., Vol. 411, pages 61-66. Springer, Berlin-New York, 1974.\n\nChvátal's conjecture and correlation inequalities\n\n  * Jan 2018\n  * 22-43\n\n  * Ehud Friedgut\n  * Jeff Kahn\n  * Gil Kalai\n  * Nathan Keller\n\nEhud Friedgut, Jeff Kahn, Gil Kalai, and Nathan Keller. Chvátal's conjecture and correlation inequalities. J. Combin. Theory Ser. A, 156:22-43, 2018.\n\nBiased halfspaces, noise sensitivity, and local Chernoff inequalities. Discrete Anal\n\n  * Jan 2019\n  * pp\n\n  * Nathan Keller\n  * Ohad Klein\n\nNathan Keller and Ohad Klein. Biased halfspaces, noise sensitivity, and local Chernoff inequalities. Discrete Anal., 2019. Paper No. 13, 50 pp.\n\nAnalysis of Boolean functions\n\n  * Jan 2014\n\n  * O' Ryan\n  * Donnell\n\nRyan O'Donnell. Analysis of Boolean functions. Cambridge University Press, New York, 2014.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInherited arcs in André planes of even order | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2606.14345)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last month; This article constructs ovals in André planes of order $q^t$q t, $q$q even and $t$t prime that are inherited from conics of the Desarguesian plane $PG(2,q^t)$P G(2,q t). ... Hui and Wen-Ai Jackson}, year={2026}, eprint={2606.14345}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14345}, }\nSubmitted 12 Jun 2026\n\n# Inherited arcs in André planes of even order\n\nSB\n\nS.G. Barwick\n\nAM\n\nAlice M.W. Hui\n\nWJ\n\nWen-Ai Jackson\n\n## Abstract\n\nAndré planes of order $q^t$q t can be constructed from the Desarguesian plane $PG(2,q^t)$P G(2,q t) using a process that involves replacing André nets. This article constructs ovals in André planes of order $q^t$q t, $q$q even and $t$t prime that are inherited from conics of the Desarguesian plane $PG(2,q^t)$P G(2,q t). Further, it is shown that certain conics do not inherit to arcs under the process of André replacement.\n\nView Paper\n\n4 views\n\nv1\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{barwick2026inheritedarcsandreplanes, title={Inherited arcs in Andr\\'e planes of even order}, author={S. G. Barwick and Alice M. W. Hui and Wen-Ai Jackson}, year={2026},