nE-Print Comment(s)  | 13 pages, comments are welcome!; http://arxiv.org/abs/2606.08958--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Chang_F in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AChang_F+in%3Amath)\nciteturn5search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Jun 09 2026 math.CO math.PR arXiv:2606.08958v1 ... @misc{2606.08958, author = {Fan Chang}, title = {{A} spectral correlation inequality for increasing {B}oolean functions}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2606.08958}, note = {arXiv:2606.08958v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n  * The sharp diagonal spectral correlation inequality on the discrete cube\n\nFan Chang, Hong Liu, Miao Liu\n\nJul 01 2026 math.CO math.FA math.PR arXiv:2606.32024v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2606.32024, author = {Fan Chang and Hong Liu and Miao Liu}, title = {{T}he sharp diagonal spectral correlation inequality on the discrete cube}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2606.32024}, note = {arXiv:2606.32024v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe prove the sharp diagonal spectral correlation conjecture of Friedgut, Kahn, Kalai and Keller, proposed in their Fourier-analytic approach to Chvátal's conjecture. For every pair of increasing Boolean functions $f,g:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}$, $$\\mathrmCov(f,g)\\ge4\\sum_\\varnothing\\ne S⊆[n]|S|\\hatf(S)^2\\hatg(S)^2.$$ Thus covariance controls the degree-weighted collision of the two nonconstant Fourier spectra, giving a sharp Fourier strengthening of the Harris--Kleitman inequality. The extremal proof uses a coordinatewise coupling at the critical bias together with an isoperimetric inequality for increasing families. The stability proof uses dual families and random ordered partitions to obtain Fourier concentration, then applies biased Friedgut--Kalai--Naor theorem to force the star structure.\n\n  * A spectral correlation inequality for increasing Boolean functions\n\nFan Chang\n\nJun 09 2026 math.CO math.PR arXiv:2606.08958v1\n\n[Button